sequenceDiagram
  %% status: draft
  %% command: proof:run
  %% kind: proof.run
  autonumber
  participant CLI as CI job / CLI
  participant GW as Gateway
  participant GH as GitHub
  participant Q as Proof queue<br/>FIFO
  participant SW as Swamp
  participant CP as CI Nodes<br/>copies · runners
  CLI->>GW: Record the proof
  %%> Record commit, authenticated caller and options, then queue admission. The proof Project is a nested-fleet Project whose tasks run as copies of its default Instance. Operator and CI proofs use the same CI places.
  GW-->>CLI: Follow one proof Operation
  GW->>GH: Select commands<br/>check trust
  %%> Read the trusted commit, selection list and cases. A fork needs approval of this head and run-proofs. No selected command succeeds without a copy. Trust passes before any copy exists: a copy holds the nested Club key, so untrusted code never reaches one.
  %%> Retry:
  %%> - A GitHub read that fails with a transport error or a 5xx answer is read once more after 2 s, once in the first admission attempt (a later attempt keeps that attempt's selection and reads GitHub no more). A rate limit, 401, 403, 404 or 422 is not read again.
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - The refusal names the read, the HTTP status or the transport error class, and the reset of a rate limit: proof.github_unavailable (run again), proof.github_rate_limited (run after the reset), proof.github_refused (an operator checks the GitHub App), proof.github_not_found (do not retry).
  opt --base supplied
    GW->>GH: Verify the ancestor base<br/>list every intervening commit
    %%> The base is exclusive and the tested commit is inclusive. Refuse a missing or non-ancestor base. An equal base selects nothing. Read each commit's changes against its first parent and its own selection list; never use the net diff of the range.
  end
  CLI->>CLI: Expand base and head<br/>with the runner
  %%> The runner validates and expands fixtures, list vars, variants and batch setup. Compare its canonical per-case definitions by id. Command code changes select the whole file. Runner, selection code or selection list changes disable narrowing for every selected command. Tooling alone selects no commands. Unknown fixture keys and any planning doubt select the whole file.
  CLI->>GH: Publish the selection<br/>for this base and head
  %%> CI consumes this same result and uploads proof-selection.json before proof admission. Include every considered file, even when it selects zero cases.
  GW->>GH: Read the runner selection<br/>for this base and head
  %%> For a range, read each intervening commit's exact first-parent comparison. Accept only the exact version, head, merge base and cases file. Missing, expired, malformed or mismatched results select the whole file. The Gateway does not compare YAML definitions.
  opt --base supplied
    GW->>GW: Union selections<br/>on the tested tree
    %%> A whole-file selection includes its current cases. Otherwise union selected ids and keep only ids still present at the tested commit. List removed files and ids as uncovered, gone at tested SHA. Trust remains on the newest exact SHA. Case receipts and execution are unchanged.
  end
  GW->>GW: Record case ids<br/>and reasons
  %%> Select added and changed cases and their variants, and users whose expanded dependencies changed. Deleted cases select nothing. Report changed, added, uses changed fixture, uses changed var, batch setup changed, command code changed or proof tooling changed. A verified zero-case selection succeeds without a copy.
  %%> A selected cases file with `cases: []`, by --all, by its command's code or by a range, selects nothing and is listed as uncovered (none: no case yet).
  GW->>GW: Plan selected cases<br/>in batches
  %%> Expand selected variant ids only. Sum measured case seconds from prior reports; unseen cases use the measured median, or one second with no history.
  GW->>Q: Join by proof Operation id
  %%> When:
  %%> - Earlier open parents without runs block both operator and CI proofs, including during selection. Ended or cancelled parents and proofs with nothing to prove leave the queue. An older parent's own 7200-second admission allowance bounds its wait.
  Q->>GW: Admit the front proof
  %%> Why:
  %%> - The Swamp Node shares one CPU core, so only one proof works at a time. Under the admission transaction, check the place before creating groups or children; record runs and the execution start time together.
  %%> When:
  %%> - An open parent with runs or an unresolved child holds the place. Stale children and their parent's run list cannot hold it alone. Parent cancellation alone cannot free it.
  %%> - A held start stays protected while its parent is open within its 7200-second allowance from creation. The watchdog cancels unreleased children without a native run when their parent ended or is missing, using normal group cleanup.
  %%> - Other parents retry every 20 seconds. While a proof holds the place, show "Waiting for proof Operation <id> to finish." as the step and capacity reason. A waiting parent reads `waiting` and has no start time; at admission it reads `running` with its start time, and the step clears. It holds no place.
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - A child that nothing ends stops holding the place after its own deadline plus the existing 60-minute stale-hold bound. Without a deadline, use its last activity plus the longest shard budget and that bound. A timed-out lock holder uses its finish time, or last activity when absent, plus the bound.
  %%> - If that cancellation is lost, a held child of an ended or missing parent stops holding the place 60 minutes after its last activity. Admission past a stale child warns after commit and gateway:status reports gateway.proof_place_stale_child. Admission never changes the stale child.
  %%> - Waiting more than 7200 seconds from creation fails proof.capacity_timeout, for both operator and CI proofs. No free CI place uses the same allowance.
  GW->>GW: Count free CI places<br/>on every CI Node
  %%> Which Nodes:
  %%> - Every Node that offers proof places, records the hold script of this release (ProofCopyHosts::takesCopies) and holds the default's current seed. The default's own Node holds it.
  %%> Places on a Node:
  %%> - Its ci_places minus its busy runners minus the proof copies already on it (any proof's; task copies take no place). No more than its MemAvailable holds whole copy peaks (proof.copy_memory). One rule, in ProofCopyHosts.
  %%> How:
  %%> - Read the runner state and MemAvailable of each Node from Prometheus before the admission transaction. A Node without a fresh read offers no place.
  %%> When:
  %%> - With no free place on any Node, the proof waits with the step "Wait for a free CI place" and tries again every 20 seconds.
  %%> - The first attempt records the selected cases and the batch plan on the proof. A later attempt keeps them: it does not check the commit, read GitHub or select again.
  GW->>GW: Balance batches<br/>by measured time
  %%> One shard per free place, at most one per part. The read-only cases (`read_only: true`) of a batch with no setup form their own batch. The weight of a part is its measured case seconds, plus 180 s for its reset and setup when it is not read-only. Longest weight first; assign each part to the least loaded copy. Record expected cases and all shard operations atomically. Each shard records its run budget: 600 s for the shard, 180 s for each part that is not read-only (at least one) and the measured case seconds, times 1.5, rounded up to five minutes, at most two hours.
  %%> Split:
  %%> - A batch is one part until a split pays. While a place is free, take the heaviest part that is not read-only and has two or more cases, and split it by case into two parts: its cases in file order, cut where the two case-second sums are closest. Keep the split only when it lowers the heaviest copy's weight; then try again. A case never splits; its stages stay together.
  %%> - Each part of a split batch is a batch run of its own on its own copy: its own reset, the batch's setup, then only its cases. A read-only batch never splits: it has no reset to pay for.
  %%> Why:
  %%> - Each reset and setup costs as much as many short cases. A weight of case seconds alone puts many short batches on one copy, and that copy becomes the tail of the proof.
  %%> - One converge or role-add batch alone took longer than the proof's 5 minutes. Its cases change the fleet but each uses its own resources, so they need the same starting state, not the same copy. A part pays 180 s for its reset; a split pays only when it shortens the longest copy.
  GW->>GW: Start one copy per shard
  %%> In the admission transaction, each shard takes one free place on a Node. Its task group starts an `instance.create` of a copy there, the same start as a task copy, and records its run child.
  %%> Seed:
  %%> - Each copy starts from its own Node's seed: the default's seed pair on the default's Node, the seed holder on a CI Node (TaskCopyStart::seed).
  SW->>CP: Stop idle runners<br/>orbit-ci-runner-hold hold
  %%> Why:
  %%> - A place runs one runner job or one proof copy. A Node's idle runners would take new jobs in the places that the proof holds.
  %%> When:
  %%> - In each copy's create, before the copy starts its inner fleet. Under one lock per Node, the copy records its hold.
  %%> How many:
  %%> - Idle runners stop only down to a limit: the running runners must not exceed ci_places minus the proof holds on the Node. Idle runners stop down to that number, and only those are recorded. With 5 places, no busy runner and 2 holds, 2 idle runners stop and 3 run.
  %%> Busy runners:
  %%> - A runner whose Runner.Worker process runs is busy. It finishes its job and is never stopped.
  GW->>GW: Hold admitted child starts
  %%> Execution copies must all succeed before the first start is queued. A child is not due until its copy is `active`: Orbit checks again every 20 seconds. A copy whose create ended without success (an expired budget only once its expiry is settled, as Swamp can still reopen it), whose completion failed, or that is gone, fails its child with proof.run_failed; its cases are unrun, and the child's end cancels its group, which destroys the copy and releases its CI place.
  par each admitted child
    GW->>SW: Start one run per copy
    %%> Queue every due start at once. A lost confirmation never permits resubmission: recovery binds the run or the child ends, bounded by the released child's 600-second admission deadline. A refusal or definite start failure of one child does not hold its siblings. Before submission, an ended or cancelled parent cancels the child through normal cancellation and group cleanup.
    %%> Why:
    %%> - A gap between starts adds to the wall time of the proof for each child, and the proof holds the only proof place meanwhile.
    %%> A held child has no native admission deadline. Release starts its normal 600-second admission budget and preserves its captured execution budget. A lost start uses the existing watchdog: after the parent's allowance, all children quiet for 300 seconds makes unstarted children fail with proof.admission_interrupted and cleans up their groups.
  end
  SW->>CP: Bring in the commit
  %%> One Ansible phase from the copy's Node, as `agent` in the copy (ADR 0268): fetch the commit by SHA from the public Orbit repository through the copy's own egress, check it out detached, and run each of the Project's development deploy steps whose inputs changed since the seed. A failed fetch fails by name after 60 s. No retry.
  %%> No mirror:
  %%> - A proof copy needs no Git mirror of the Project on its Node, so a CI Node keeps none. Its clone brings no branch into a Node mirror; this fetch is the only way the commit comes in, on every Node.
  SW->>SW: Check the workspace commit
  %%> The workspace is at the commit under proof, or the run fails with proof.workspace_commit_mismatch before any case. Each batch's reset restarts and migrates the nested Gateway on this checkout, and the runner applies its grants and collections, so the fleet runs the tested code.
  loop each assigned part, read-only batches first
    SW->>CP: Read the command batches
    SW->>SW: Select the assigned part<br/>batch and case ids
    opt first part, or the part before it could change the fleet
      SW->>CP: Reset the inner fleet
      %%> A read-only batch leaves the fleet at `base`, so the part after it needs no reset. The runner refuses a case marked `read_only: true` that has a setup or a step that can change the fleet.
      %%> `base` is the seed's fleet converged with the default scope at the seed's commit (the refresh's default converge). So a converge or role-add case finds nothing to change unless the commit under proof or the case's own setup changed an input.
    end
    SW->>CP: Run batch setup
    SW->>CP: Run selected cases<br/>of the part
    %%> A copy runs no replay of flagged cases (`fails_on_base: true`) on the merge base: the head's verdicts are the run's.
    CP-->>SW: Verdict lines
    %%> The Gateway records the verdict lines of each batch on its shard as the batch ends. A shard that ends at its run budget keeps them; only the cases of the batches that did not end are unrun.
  end
  SW-->>GW: Report verdicts<br/>full artifact
  %%> Capture the full report, not its 4 KiB history tail. Identify command and file on each line. Missing, duplicate or unexpected case verdicts prevent a pass; a missing expected case is unrun.
  GW->>GW: Remove the copy
  %%> Every run end (passed, failed, cancelled, timed out, interrupted, never started) cancels its group with discard, which destroys the copy. A busy copy is asked again; the watchdog asks again for every group of a proof Operation that still owns a copy.
  SW->>CP: Start held runners<br/>orbit-ci-runner-hold release
  %%> When:
  %%> - In each copy's destroy, so after every run end, also a failure or a cancel. Under the Node's lock the copy drops its hold.
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - When no hold is left on the Node, start exactly the runners that the holds stopped, and no other. A runner that is gone is skipped. A second release changes nothing.
  GW->>GW: Merge all shard verdicts
  %%> Wait for every admitted child, require every expected case and every selected command, and close one proof result. Preserve a native interrupted shard as inconclusive interrupted coverage, even with all passed rows. A completed failed case or a non-interrupted child that did not pass takes precedence and fails the proof. Keep every case verdict, including synthesized unrun rows. Cancellation and admission interruption keep their existing failure semantics.
  GW-->>CLI: One merged report
  CLI->>CLI: Require the proof verdict
  %%> The proof job is in Required checks. A selected command with a failed case or no verdict fails. An interrupted result stays interrupted in the receipt and exits nonzero, including with all passed rows. Malformed coverage or a nonzero CLI exit never passes. No selected command passes. Capacity is only a bounded wait, never a successful no-case result.
  %%> A fork uses the outside proof's status on this exact head. A missing, pending or failed status fails Required checks; rerun the failed Required job after the approved outside proof succeeds.
